WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.952 --> 00:00:05.904
[EXCITING MUSIC]

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:17.380
This is one of the most dramatic skulls

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:19.930
from our prehistoric planet.

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:24.490
It belonged to a plant-eating dinosaur called triceratops.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:30.470
[ROAR]

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:34.490
This dinosaur is instantly recognizable

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:37.325
from the massive frill at the back of its head.

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:45.090
But 140 years after the first fossil was found,

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:50.330
scientists are still asking what was the frill for.

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:54.500
These are enormous structures.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:57.310
So it's an enormous investment in bone and tissue

00:00:57.310 --> 00:01:01.270
and also in the muscular effort to hold these structures up.

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:03.670
All of this investment must mean that these features

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:06.920
are very important to the animal's way of life.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:07.460
But how?

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:14.390
Evidence of injury on triceratops fossils

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:17.870
suggest that the frills were important in defense

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:21.020
and combat.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.960
We can see lesions on the skull, which

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:28.150
are areas where the skull has been damaged and healed.

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:31.210
We can also see where a predator has

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:33.400
taken a chunk out of a frill.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:35.620
That might indicate that we're looking

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:37.600
at some sort of defensive structure.

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:43.290
But the story doesn't end there.

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:50.610
Another possibility is that the frill was used

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.560
to attract prospective mates.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.030
We see this in living animals where structures like horns

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:01.160
show off how healthy they are and who's

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:03.860
a good prospect for producing lots of young.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.880
For a moose, having large antlers

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:13.210
makes the male more attractive--

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:15.910
vital when it reaches sexual maturity.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:24.860
Could the same apply to the triceratops' frill?

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:28.190
If it was only for defense, then we would expect to see them

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:31.100
develop to the same degree in juveniles as in adults,

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:33.475
but we don't.

00:02:33.475 --> 00:02:35.690
We see them being much smaller in the juveniles

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:38.690
and not really developing into the very, very large structures

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:42.590
of the adults until much later.

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:45.140
This isn't the only evidence that they

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:48.690
were used in sexual display.

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:52.270
The surfaces of the frills are very heavily grooved.

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:54.960
These grooves probably carried nerves and blood vessels

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:59.830
that would have supplied the skin growing over those frills.

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:02.470
This may have been very brightly colored, a big surface

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:05.245
area for the animal to show off to potential mates.

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:17.910
Scientists now believe that the triceratops' best known feature

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:22.220
was truly multipurpose--

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:31.400
very useful for attack and defense but also ideal

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:32.570
for attracting a mate.

00:03:37.977 --> 00:03:39.380
[ROAR]

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:42.170
It would have been an awe inspiring sight

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:44.420
to friend or foe.

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:47.470
[EXCITING MUSIC]

